<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional implementation of SimE</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Prototype extractor.</span>
In image classification, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, setting the average features of the classes as the weights of the classifier. For the <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task (<math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>), we do not update the weights of <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A SimE formulation ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>); instead, we use the <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> directly to calculate the average value of features and set it as prototypes in datasets <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{x}_{i}^{1},\ldots,\bm{x}_{i}^{t}\}_{i=1}^{n_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{x}_{i}^{1},\ldots,\bm{x}_{i}^{t}\}_{i=1}^{n_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{|D^{t}|}I(y_{j}=k)E(\bm{x})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{|D^{t}|}I(y_{j}=k)E(\bm{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the prototype of increment class <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task, <math id="A1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\sum_{j=1}^{\lvert|D^{t}|\rvert}I(y_{j}=k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\sum_{j=1}^{\lvert|D^{t}|\rvert}I(y_{j}=k)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. <math id="A1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the average features of class <math id="A1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that the images of class <math id="A1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> should exhibit the greatest similarity with <math id="A1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> among all prototypes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Classifier</span>.
In CIL, the classifier is dynamic and can be implemented in various ways<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. In this paper, we use a FC layer as our classifier<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. For the <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task, the classifier is an FC layer <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times(N+M)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times(N+M)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the feature dimension, <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes at (<math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-1)-th task, <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of increment classes in <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task. We use the training set data <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\text{pro}}=\{(\bm{x}_{i}^{t},\bm{y}_{i}^{t})\}_{i=1}^{n_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mtext>pro</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\text{pro}}=\{(\bm{x}_{i}^{t},\bm{y}_{i}^{t})\}_{i=1}^{n_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task to calculate the prototype <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{pro}}=\text{mean}(E(\bm{x}_{\text{pro}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>pro</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mtext>pro</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{pro}}=\text{mean}(E(\bm{x}_{\text{pro}}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples in the training set of the <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task, then update the FC layer <math id="A1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t}=W_{t-1}+W_{\text{pro}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>pro</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t}=W_{t-1}+W_{\text{pro}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{pro}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>pro</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{pro}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>. The cosine similarity for classification is then calculated as:</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{x})=\left(\frac{\mathbf{W}}{\|\mathbf{W}\|_{2}}\right)^{\top}\left(\frac{E(\bm{x})}{\|E(\bm{x})\|_{2}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{x})=\left(\frac{\mathbf{W}}{\|\mathbf{W}\|_{2}}\right)^{\top}\left(\frac{E(\bm{x})}{\|E(\bm{x})\|_{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">given that prototype <math id="A1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is most similar to instances of class <math id="A1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, it is expected that the classifier will assign a higher probability to the correct class label.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The toy model</span>. In SimE, there are numerous implementations for the encoder and adapter. Here, we first employ the CLIP visual encoder as the encoder and AdaptFormer or Multi-Adapter as the adapter to establish a toy model of SimE. Subsequently, we explore various specific implementations of SimE.
<span id="A1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> is a powerful visual-language contrastive learning model comprising an image encoder and a text encoder. It is trained on 400 million image-text pairs and possesses strong feature extraction capabilities. In this paper, we employ the CLIP pre-trained visual encoder as our backbone, i.e., we instantiate our encoder using the CLIP pre-trained weights <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, there exist CLIP models pre-trained on different datasets, corresponding to different encoder instances. It is noteworthy that during the continual learning process, the pre-trained weights of CLIP are frozen and do not participate in weight updates.
<span id="A1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptformer<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="A1.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">[</span><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a><span id="A1.p3.1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">]</span></cite></span> containing a down-projection <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{down}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{down}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times R}</annotation></semantics></math> to reduce the feature dimension, a non-linear activation function(ReLU) and an up-projection <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{up}}\in\mathbb{R}^{R\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{up}}\in\mathbb{R}^{R\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> to project the features back to their original dimension, where <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the feature dimension and <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of bottleneck. The specific form of the AdaptFormer is:</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(\tilde{\eta_{i}},\bm{h}_{i})=\alpha\mathrm{ReLU}(\bm{h}_{i}\cdot W_{\text{down}})\cdot W_{\text{up}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ReLU</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(\tilde{\eta_{i}},\bm{h}_{i})=\alpha\mathrm{ReLU}(\bm{h}_{i}\cdot W_{\text{down}})\cdot W_{\text{up}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the scaling factor in the residual connection, which is set to 0.1 by default in AdapterFormer.
<span id="A1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Adapter</span>
is represented by (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In III-B Multi-Adapter formulation ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The Adaptformer is a special case in Multi-Adapter and we employ the same down-up projects in (<a href="#A1.E14" title="In Appendix A Additional implementation of SimE ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) to initialize the Multi-Adapter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional optimisation of adapter for domain adaptation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The CLIP possesses exceptional zero-shot capabilities, enabling it to achieve performance comparable to supervised models on new datasets without finetuning. This underscores CLIP’s powerful feature extraction abilities. However, CLIP still faces a domain gap between pre-trained datasets and downstream task datasets. For instance, while CLIP excels on datasets like ImageNet, its performance on MNIST<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> is poor. To bridge this gap, it is necessary to finetune CLIP for incremental learning downstream tasks. During the finetuning process, the weights <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i},\phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i},\phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of pre-trained CLIP image encoder <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}(\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}(\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A SimE formulation ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) are frozen, and only the adapters and classifier are updated:</p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{*}(\bm{c})=F(E^{\prime}(\bm{c}),D)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{*}(\bm{c})=F(E^{\prime}(\bm{c}),D),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the adapted CLIP image encoder, <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the finetuning process, <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> represents the data of the incremental tasks, <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the trainable parameters, <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta=\cup_{j=1}^{Z}\tilde{\eta}_{ij}\cup\theta_{W_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\cup_{j=1}^{Z}\tilde{\eta}_{ij}\cup\theta_{W_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>
Through finetuning, the pre-trained encoder can better adapt to downstream datasets. However, continuously finetuning on a series of tasks <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{D_{1},\ldots,D_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{D_{1},\ldots,D_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> would diminish its feature extraction capabilities due to Catastrophic Forgetting. Therefore, in this paper, we finetune the pre-trained encoder only on the first task <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to maximise the retention of the encoder’s previous knowledge:</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{*}(\bm{c})=F(E^{\prime}(\bm{c}_{1}),D_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{*}(\bm{c})=F(E^{\prime}(\bm{c}_{1}),D_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By finetuning the first task, the encoder can better adapt to downstream datasets. However, the finetuning process will inevitably diminish the zero-shot capabilities of the pre-trained encoder. To better preserve the feature extraction capabilities of the pre-trained encoder, we concatenate the features output by the pre-trained encoder and the finetuned encoder and feed this combined feature set into the classifier for image classification.</p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{c}(\bm{c})=\{E^{*}(\bm{c});E(\bm{c})\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{c}(\bm{c})=\{E^{*}(\bm{c});E(\bm{c})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{c}(\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{c}(\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the composite encoder, and <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\cdot;\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\cdot;\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the concatenation of the features output by the pre-trained encoder and the finetuned encoder. It is important to note that the concatenation is performed after finetuning, and encoder <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{E}^{c}(\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{E}^{c}(\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math> will be frozen in subsequent tasks to maximise the retention of its feature extraction capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional theorem proof</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Monotonic Bound on Performance) ‣ III-C Parameter spaces and function properties ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Proof" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Proof</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}\in\Theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}\in\Theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a corresponding <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{N}\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{N}\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the newly introduced Adapter parameters are set to the “identity” or “off”. Hence</p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\bigl(x;\,\theta_{0},\,0,\,\varnothing\bigr)\;=\;f\bigl(x;\,\theta_{N},\,N,\,\mathrm{loc}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\bigl(x;\,\theta_{0},\,0,\,\varnothing\bigr)\;=\;f\bigl(x;\,\theta_{N},\,N,\,\mathrm{loc}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which implies</p>
<table id="A3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\theta_{0}\in\Theta_{0}}f(\theta_{0})\;\;\leq\;\;\sup_{\theta_{N}\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta_{N})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.560em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.560em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\theta_{0}\in\Theta_{0}}f(\theta_{0})\;\;\leq\;\;\sup_{\theta_{N}\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta_{N})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}\subseteq\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}\subseteq\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="A3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\theta_{N}\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta_{N})\;\;\leq\;\;\sup_{\theta_{M}\in\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta_{M})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.560em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.560em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\theta_{N}\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta_{N})\;\;\leq\;\;\sup_{\theta_{M}\in\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta_{M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Combining these two inequalities finishes the proof:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup f(\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}})\geq\sup f(\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}})\geq\sup f(\Theta_{0})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup f(\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}})\geq\sup f(\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}})\geq\sup f(\Theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Non-monotonic Actual Solutions) ‣ III-C Parameter spaces and function properties ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Proof" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Proof</annotation></semantics></math>.  Formally, for performance measure function with any parameters</p>
<table id="A3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{num,loc}\to\mathbb{R}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{num,loc}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">It will be affected by both <math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="num" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">num</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="loc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">loc</annotation></semantics></math> of adapters. If we consider adapter connect combination methods as <math id="A3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\Theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\Theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no guarantee that <math id="A3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\Theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\Theta)</annotation></semantics></math> will exactly return <math id="A3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\max_{\Theta}f(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\max_{\Theta}f(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a result, for <math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;N</annotation></semantics></math>, we only have</p>
<table id="A3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta)&gt;\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>loc</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_{M,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta)&gt;\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_{N,\mathrm{loc}}}f(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">but it is possible that</p>
<table id="A3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(T_{1}(\Theta_{M}))&lt;f(T_{2}(\Theta_{N}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(T_{1}(\Theta_{M}))&lt;f(T_{2}(\Theta_{N})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This precisely reflects the practical scenario described in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Non-monotonic Actual Solutions) ‣ III-C Parameter spaces and function properties ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional experimental setting </h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
The main experiments are implemented on CIFAR100 and TinyImageNet. CIFAR100<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> consist of 60K images with size of <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> from 100 classes, which are split into 2 classes, 5 classes and 10 classes in each step. Each class consist of 500 training and 100 testing samples. TinyImageNet<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, as a subset of ImageNet, consists of 100K images with size of <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> from 200 classes and each class consist of 500 training and 50 testing images. In our experiment, the 100 classes of TinyImageNet are split as base classes which are used for finetuning, and the rest 100 classes are split into 5 classes, 10 classes and 20 classes in each step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate scalability and robustness in more realistic and larger-scale scenarios, we further conduct experiments on: CUB200 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, containing 11,788 bird images across 200 classes, with resolutions typically exceeding 300 pixels on the long side; ImageNet-R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, which contains 30K images from 200 ImageNet classes rendered in artistic, cartoon, and abstract styles; ImageNet-100, a 100-class subset of ImageNet with images of typical resolution exceeding 500 pixels; VTAB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, a collection of 19 diverse vision datasets with images resized to 224×224; ImageNet-A <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, an adversarially filtered subset of ImageNet containing 7.5K challenging images with long sides typically above 500 pixels; ObjectNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, which has 50K real-world object images with significant viewpoint, background, and rotation variations (long sides usually over 500 pixels); and OmniBenchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, a broad-spectrum benchmark with diverse image sizes, some exceeding 300×500 pixels. In this paper, we sample 200 classes from ObjectNet and ImageNet-A, and 5 datasets from VTAB, with each containing 10 classes, to conduct CIL experiments. All datasets in Table.<a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-B Multi-Adapter formulation ‣ III The SimE framework via vision-language models with adapters ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> are split into 10 steps, except
ObjectNet, which is split into 20 steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Network architectures in the SimE.</span>
We have developed SimE, leveraging CLIP and adapters (Adaptformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and Multi-Adapter) for class-incremental learning tasks. Within SimE, the CLIP image processor is utilized for data preprocessing. The image encoder, featuring various backbone sizes such as ViT-B/16, ViT-B/32, and ViT-L/14, is finetuned using adapters across different pre-trained datasets, including WIT-400B, Laion-400M, Laion-2B, Datacomp-1B, and CommonPool-1B. The classifier employs a fully connected (FC) layer, which uses class prototypes as weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation metrics.</span>
Following the methodology of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, we assess SimE and compare it with other baseline methods using two metrics: Average Accuracy (<span id="A4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg</span>) and Last Accuracy (<span id="A4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Last</span>). <span id="A4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg</span> represents the mean of the Top-1 accuracy for every task, while <span id="A4.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Last</span> denotes the Top-1 accuracy of final task. Mathematically, for the <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task, Average Accuracy is calculated as follows: <span id="A4.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg<math id="A4.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>Last<sub id="A4.p4.1.6.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A4.p4.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sub></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Others CIL methods.</span>
We compare the SimE with existing CLIP-based methods (e.g., CoOp<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, ZSCL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, Continual-CLIP<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, AttriCLIP<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, Boosting-CL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, L2P<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, DualPrompt<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, CODA-Prompt<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, APER<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>) and typical continual learning methods (e.g., LwF<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, iCaRL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, DER<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, iTAML<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, ARI<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, UCIR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, PASS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, and DyTox<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training procedures.</span>
In this study, our experiments utilize the image encoder from CLIP<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, and all experiments use 12 adapters (one per transformer block) unless stated otherwise. We finetune the SimE over 20 epochs on the first task for every dataset. Subsequently, all model weights, except the classifier, remain unchanged. During finetuning, we employ Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) as the optimizer. The starting learning rate is set at 0.01, adhering to a cosine decay schedule. We apply a weight decay of 0.0005, a batch size of 64, and the adapter’s bottleneck dimension is set to 64.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional comparison on accuracy</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present additional accuracy comparisons of different CIL methods and datasets. To ensure fairness in backbone comparisons, we evaluated all methods using the same CLIP ViT-B/16 backbone to maintain a consistent basis for comparison. As shown in Table.<a href="#A0.T6" title="TABLE VI ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, our method consistently outperforms the baselines in most cases, even under identical backbone settings. This confirms that the observed performance gains are not solely attributable to the backbone capacity, but rather to the effectiveness of our proposed learning strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Class imbalance is a significant challenge in real-world continual learning scenarios. To investigate its impact, we conducted additional experiments under imbalanced settings. The results are reported in Table.<a href="#A0.T7" title="TABLE VII ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a>, where <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="imb\_factor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">imb\_factor</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the imbalance ratio. Specifically, the number of samples for class <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="max\_num*(imb\_factor^{(i/(num\_classes)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">max\_num*(imb\_factor^{(i/(num\_classes)})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="max\_num" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">max\_num</annotation></semantics></math> is the original number of samples per class in the balanced setting, and <math id="A5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="num\_classes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">num\_classes</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of classes. The results in Table.<a href="#A0.T7" title="TABLE VII ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> show that class imbalance indeed leads to a substantial drop in performance. In particular, when the imbalance factor decreases from 1 to 0.01 (i.e., from a fully balanced distribution to a highly imbalanced one), the model’s accuracy drops by up to 7.06%. This confirms that class imbalance poses a considerable challenge for incremental learning models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Besides, Table.<a href="#A0.T8" title="TABLE VIII ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a> and Fig.<a href="#A0.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> show the Last accuracy for each task over 10 steps on CIFAR-100, compared with other CIL methods. We also compare the performance of our method on CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet over 10 and 20 steps, as reported in Fig.<a href="#A0.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional influence of adapter components in SimE via ablation studies</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we report additional experiments on the influence of adapter components in SimE. We first study the influence of adapter connections between transformer blocks and report it in Table.<a href="#A0.T9" title="TABLE IX ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IX</span></a> &amp; Table.<a href="#A0.T10" title="TABLE X ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">X</span></a>, where ”CLIP” indicates no adapter inserted in transformer blocks. All the experiments are conducted on CIFAR100 with CLIP ViT-B/16.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also investigate the influence of adapter connections within transformer blocks, as illustrated in Fig.<a href="#A0.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> &amp;Table.<a href="#A0.T12" title="TABLE XII ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XII</span></a> &amp;Table.<a href="#A0.T11" title="TABLE XI ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XI</span></a>. Fig.<a href="#A0.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents the model performance on classes 70-100 during advanced steps (50 steps) with various Multi-Adapter implementations. Table.<a href="#A0.T12" title="TABLE XII ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XII</span></a> reports the results of different Multi-Adapter implementations across all steps, with the first row indicating the absence of an adapter in the encoder. Furthermore, we examine the influence of adapter’s bottleneck dimension within the Multi-Adapter framework and report results in Table.<a href="#A0.T11" title="TABLE XI ‣ A Simple Efficiency Incremental Learning Framework via Vision-Language Model with Nonlinear Multi-Adapters" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XI</span></a> with experiments conducted on Adapt-Atten.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
